## Part 2 — Trend analytics

Regex gives you fields. These give you findings.

### H1 — Password spray

Many accounts, few attempts each, one source, short window. Inverts the per-account
lockout threshold, which is exactly why it evades default alerting.

```spl
<4625 base search + extractions>
| bin _time span=1h
| stats dc(TargetUser) AS users_tried, count AS attempts, values(failure_reason) AS reasons BY _time, SrcIp
| eval attempts_per_user=round(attempts/users_tried,2)
| where users_tried >= 10 AND attempts_per_user <= 3
| sort - users_tried
```

**Then check whether it worked** — the step most spray hunts skip:

```spl
<4624 base search> 
| search SrcIp IN (<spray source from above>)
| stats min(_time) AS first_success, values(TargetUser) AS compromised BY SrcIp
```

A 4625 storm followed by a 4624 from the same source is the finding. The storm alone is
just noise.

### H2 — Account enumeration

```spl
<4625 base search + extractions>
| where SubStatus="0xC0000064"
| bin _time span=1h
| stats dc(TargetUser) AS nonexistent_accounts, values(TargetUser) AS sample BY _time, SrcIp, Workstation
| where nonexistent_accounts >= 15
```

`Workstation` is worth keeping — attacker tooling often sets a static or obviously
synthetic workstation name, and that becomes a durable pivot.

### H3 — First-ever source for an account

Rarity beats threshold for initial access. This finds an account authenticating from
somewhere it never has.

```spl
<4624 base search + extractions>
| where LogonType IN ("3","8","10")
| stats min(_time) AS first_seen, max(_time) AS last_seen, count BY TargetUser, SrcIp
| eventstats min(first_seen) AS account_first_ever BY TargetUser
| where first_seen > relative_time(now(), "-7d") AND first_seen != account_first_ever
| convert ctime(first_seen) ctime(last_seen)
| sort first_seen
```

Run once over 90 days to build intuition for your baseline before scheduling it.

### H4 — Impossible / anomalous RDP

```spl
<4624 base search + extractions>
| where LogonType="10"
| where NOT (cidrmatch("10.0.0.0/8", SrcIp) OR cidrmatch("172.16.0.0/12", SrcIp) OR cidrmatch("192.168.0.0/16", SrcIp))
| stats count, values(TargetUser) AS users, dc(host) AS hosts_reached BY SrcIp
| sort - count
```

External RDP success is a finding on its own merits. Corroborate with the Terminal
Services LocalSessionManager operational log (events 21, 25) and RemoteConnectionManager
1149, both on by default.

### H5 — Off-hours authentication

```spl
<4624 base search + extractions>
| where LogonType IN ("3","10")
| eval hour=strftime(_time,"%H"), dow=strftime(_time,"%w")
| where (hour < 6 OR hour > 20) OR dow IN ("0","6")
| stats count, values(SrcIp) AS sources, values(host) AS targets BY TargetUser
| where count >= 3
```

Noisy in shops with real shift work or overseas teams. Baseline per account rather than
globally if that describes you.

### H6 — Service install as persistence

```spl
<7045 base search + extractions>
| eval suspicious=case(
    match(ServiceFile, "(?i)\\\\(Temp|Users\\\\Public|ProgramData|AppData|Windows\\\\Temp)\\\\"), "path",
    match(ServiceFile, "(?i)(powershell|cmd\.exe|wscript|cscript|rundll32|mshta|regsvr32)"), "interpreter",
    match(ServiceFile, "(?i)-enc|-e\s|frombase64|IEX|DownloadString"), "encoded",
    match(ServiceName, "^[a-zA-Z0-9]{8,16}$") AND NOT match(ServiceName, "(?i)(update|sync|agent|monitor)"), "random name",
    1=1, null())
| where isnotnull(suspicious)
| table _time, host, ServiceName, ServiceFile, StartType, suspicious
```

PsExec, Impacket `smbexec`, and Cobalt Strike's service jump all surface here. This is
your single best execution-adjacent detection without 4688.

### H7 — Firewall: scanning preceding a logon

The one query that ties the two data sources together.

```spl
<firewall deny base search>
| bin _time span=1h
| stats dc(dest_port) AS ports_probed, dc(dest_ip) AS hosts_probed, count AS denies BY _time, src_ip
| where ports_probed >= 20 OR hosts_probed >= 25
| eval scan_time=_time
| join type=left src_ip
    [ search <4624 base search + extractions>
    | rename SrcIp AS src_ip
    | stats min(_time) AS logon_time, values(TargetUser) AS users, values(host) AS targets BY src_ip ]
| where isnotnull(logon_time) AND logon_time > scan_time AND (logon_time - scan_time) < 86400
| convert ctime(scan_time) ctime(logon_time)
```

Scanning followed by a successful authentication from the same address inside 24 hours.
This is as close to "initial access" as deny-only logs can get you.

### H8 — Firewall: the denial gap

Counterintuitive but useful. A source that generates sustained denies and then stops
either gave up or found an allowed path. The second case is invisible in deny logs,
which is precisely why the silence matters.

```spl
<firewall deny base search>
| bin _time span=1h
| stats count AS denies BY _time, src_ip
| streamstats window=6 avg(denies) AS trailing_avg BY src_ip
| where denies=0 AND trailing_avg > 50
```

Treat as a hunting prompt, not an alert. Pair with H7 against the same source.

### H9 — Blocked egress from a workstation

Post-compromise, but often the first thing you actually see.

```spl
<firewall deny base search>
| where cidrmatch("10.0.0.0/8", src_ip)
| where NOT (cidrmatch("10.0.0.0/8", dest_ip) OR cidrmatch("172.16.0.0/12", dest_ip) OR cidrmatch("192.168.0.0/16", dest_ip))
| stats count AS attempts, dc(dest_ip) AS destinations, values(dest_port) AS ports,
        earliest(_time) AS first, latest(_time) AS last BY src_ip
| eval duration_hr=round((last-first)/3600,1)
| eval rate=round(attempts/duration_hr,1)
| where attempts >= 50 AND destinations <= 5
| convert ctime(first) ctime(last)
```

Few destinations, high count, sustained over time — beacon shape. A workstation
repeatedly trying to reach one external address on a blocked port is worth a look
regardless of what the rest of this finds.

### H10 — 4648 anomaly

```spl
<4648 base search + extractions>
| eval same=if(lower(SubjectUser)==lower(UsedAccount),1,0)
| where same=0
| stats count, dc(TargetServer) AS servers, values(TargetServer) AS targets BY host, SubjectUser, UsedAccount
| where servers >= 3
```

One account explicitly using another's credentials against several servers is the
classic post-access lateral pattern, and 4648 is on by default.

